\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces The blue dots represent the total GW signal of the simulated DWDs hosted by the $66,568$ galaxies of the estimated potential gravitational wave emitting galaxies, whereas the orange dots represent the signal considering only the $20,246$ of the GWGC after dropping the galaxies with incomplete information, and without considering the ZOA. In Both cases, the PSDs are binned in $\sim 8\times 10^{-9}Hz$ large frequency bins, representing LISA's spectral resolution for an observational time $T_{obs}=4yr$. Every dot represents the signal of a frequency bin, calculated by summing the Power Spectral Density (PSD) of every galaxy that it contained, using~\eqref  {eq: total PSD}. The dots, plotted on LISA's sensitivity curve, as found in~\cite  {Robson_2019}, are about one order of magnitude below the minimum threshold of this curve and a slight difference in height is visible between the blue and the orange dot's distributions, showing the effect of considering a significantly different number of sources. This results suggest that we should not expect LISA to be able to detect the unresolved extragalactic DWDs confusion foreground, at least under the assumptions and approximations adopted in this work, and suggests that the results do not depend significantly from the number of galaxies.}}{44}{figure.3.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig: Final results plot}{{3.4}{44}{The blue dots represent the total GW signal of the simulated DWDs hosted by the $66,568$ galaxies of the estimated potential gravitational wave emitting galaxies, whereas the orange dots represent the signal considering only the $20,246$ of the GWGC after dropping the galaxies with incomplete information, and without considering the ZOA. In Both cases, the PSDs are binned in $\sim 8\times 10^{-9}Hz$ large frequency bins, representing LISA's spectral resolution for an observational time $T_{obs}=4yr$. Every dot represents the signal of a frequency bin, calculated by summing the Power Spectral Density (PSD) of every galaxy that it contained, using~\eqref {eq: total PSD}. The dots, plotted on LISA's sensitivity curve, as found in~\cite {Robson_2019}, are about one order of magnitude below the minimum threshold of this curve and a slight difference in height is visible between the blue and the orange dot's distributions, showing the effect of considering a significantly different number of sources. This results suggest that we should not expect LISA to be able to detect the unresolved extragalactic DWDs confusion foreground, at least under the assumptions and approximations adopted in this work, and suggests that the results do not depend significantly from the number of galaxies}{figure.3.4}{}}
